nna hold a backstory  but it brought in this idea of okay the  name of the hero we're gonna keep  because people know that and they like  it but we're gonna put somebody else in  that costume they're gonna have a  different thing going for them and that  continued on from there and I think by  and large DC did it best and I think it  was partly because they just kind of  stumbled into it it wasn't necessarily  by design for a lot of it and then as  time went along there was a lot more  legacy characters as as DC sort of moved  its way forward obviously there were a  lot more Green Lantern's Barry Allen  eventually died during crisis on  Infinite Earths to be replaced by Wally  West  Batgirl ended up becoming a legacy  character when Barbara Gordon became  paralyzed and the mantle of Batgirl got  taken up by somebody else and in general  what I like about legacy characters is  it allows you for a few things the first  thing it allows you for clear sectioning  off of different eras of a character  because any character that's been around  for a long time generally there's gonna  be different eras and by and large you  have to kind of section them off by  creative team problem with that is you  know creative teams can sometimes have  be very short-lived you know so short  that you wouldn't even call you know  some writers times on a character an era  you know you barely call it a run so  doing it by a span especially if you're  not a hardcore comics fan you know you  might not even know the writer off the  top of your head half the time so that  it's possible to section off by writer  and there's a validity to that but I  think there's an ease of sectioning  things off by who's holding the mantle  of that particular  character and the other and the reason I  like that is because as I'm getting  older I'm finding myself I'm finding it  harder to continue to find the joy in  comic books and it's not for some like  stupid book start as good as they were  when I was readin